<misterromdescription>
    <name>Raiden Fighters (Taiwan, single board)</name>
    <setname>rdfts</setname>
    <rbf>SeibuSPI</rbf>
    <mameversion>0277</mameversion>
    <year>1996</year>
    <manufacturer>Seibu Kaihatsu (Explorer System Corp. license)</manufacturer>
    <players>2</players>
    <joystick>8</joystick>
    <rotation>vertical (cw)</rotation>
    <region>Taiwan</region>
    <category>Shooter</category>

    <!--
      Part order is significant. rtl/rom_loader.sv walks this list with a fixed
      table and performs MAME's ROM_LOAD24_* / ROM_LOAD32_* byte scatter in
      hardware, because MRA <interleave> cannot express the 3-byte period the
      Seibu "D + P" graphics ROM pairs need. Do not reorder or insert parts
      without updating the table in rom_loader.sv to match.

      Layout produced in SDRAM (see rtl/spi_defs.vh):
        0x0000000  2 MB   386 program
        0x0200000  256 KB Z80 program
        0x0240000  192 KB text tiles
        0x0280000  2 MB   YMF271 samples
        0x0480000  6 MB   background tiles
        0x0A80000  12 MB  sprites, three 4 MB plane-pair chunks
    -->
    <!--
      These are MAME's rdfts filenames. rdfts is a clone of rdft, so in a merged
      set most of these files are stored under the PARENT's names. That is fine:
      MiSTer matches parts by the crc= attribute first (zip_search_by_crc in
      Main_MiSTer's FileOpenZip) and only falls back to the filename, so every
      part below resolves out of rdft.zip regardless of how it is named there.
      Keep the CRCs correct - they are load-bearing, not documentation.
    -->
    <!--
      Config blob, index 1. Byte 0 is the mod byte and 0x00 is what rdfts wants:
      the SXX2E single board, part table 0, every part copied rather than
      decoded. That is also the core's power-up state, so this element changes
      nothing on a fresh load - it is here so that loading rdfts AFTER a set
      that does set something (rdft's 0x01, rdft2's 0x03 plus a codec) cannot
      leave a stale value behind if the RBF is not reloaded in between.

      It must precede the index-0 ROM: Main_MiSTer sends <rom> elements in file
      order, and the loader has to know its table before the image starts.
    -->
    <rom index="1"><part>00</part></rom>

    <rom index="0" address="0x30000000" zip="rdfts.zip|rdft.zip" md5="none">
        <!-- 386 program -->
        <part name="seibu_1.u0259"        crc="e278dddd"/>
        <part name="raiden-f_prg2.u0258"  crc="58ccb10c"/>
        <part name="raiden-f_prg34.u0262" crc="63f01d17"/>

        <!-- Z80 program -->
        <part name="seibu_zprg.u1139"     crc="c1fda3e8"/>

        <!-- text layer -->
        <part name="raiden-f_fix.u0535"   crc="2be2936b"/>
        <part name="seibu_fix2.u0528"     crc="4d87e1ea"/>

        <!-- background tiles -->
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg1-d.u0526" crc="6a68054c"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg1-p.u0531" crc="3400794a"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg2-d.u0534" crc="61cd2991"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg2-p.u0530" crc="502d5799"/>

        <!-- sprites -->
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-1.u0322" crc="59d86c99"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-2.u0324" crc="1ceb0b6f"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-3.u0323" crc="36e93234"/>

        <!-- YMF271 samples -->
        <part name="raiden-f_pcm2.u0975"  crc="3f8d4a48"/>
    </rom>

    <!--
      The save file, in /media/fat/config/nvram/. 512 bytes: the DS2404's
      bookkeeping SRAM, and nothing else.

      The cartridge MRAs declare 516 here - the sample flash's four-byte region
      stamp, then these same 512 bytes as the tail. SXX2E's samples are a real
      ROM, so there is no stamp to keep and the tail sits at offset 0 instead. rom_loader's
      mod byte is what tells the core which of the two shapes to expect
      (spi_nvram.sv's `has_flash`), and `make check-mra` holds the size here
      against it.

      It is byte-for-byte MAME's own ~/.mame/nvram/rdfts/ds2404, which is the
      only nvram file MAME writes for this set.
    -->
    <nvram index="2" size="512"/>

    <!--
      INPUTS (0x604) and SYSTEM (0x60C) are active low on hardware; the core
      inverts them.

      The names below map to joystick bits 4 upwards IN ORDER, and SeibuSPI.sv
      decodes them at exactly those positions - bit 4 Shot ... bit 10 Test.
      Change one and you must change the other.

      Test is MAME's PORT_SERVICE_NO_TOGGLE: a momentary panel button, held
      down rather than latched. It is also on the DIP page below for setups
      with no spare pad button.

      The last two names are not board inputs: Pause (bit 11) gates the 386
      while the video engines keep running, and Savestate (bit 12) is the
      modifier the savestate d-pad combinations are built on - SS+Left/Right
      picks the slot, SS+Down saves, SS+Up loads. Savestate has no default=
      entry, because Main only accepts the eight base pad names there and the
      first eight buttons use all of them, so it must be bound by hand.
    -->
    <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,Button 3,Start,Coin,Service Coin,Test,Pause,Savestate (SS)"
             default="A,B,X,Start,Select,R,L,Y" count="3"/>

    <!--
      SW1:1 is the board's only DIP. The game reads it from INPUTS bit 15 and
      flips its own rendering; the core just presents the bit.

      Note the polarity: MAME's PORT_SPECIAL_ONOFF_DIPLOC(0x8000, 0x0000, ...)
      makes Off = bit clear and On = bit set, and a DIPSWITCH field is NOT
      subject to the port's IP_ACTIVE_LOW inversion the way the buttons are. So
      unlike most arcade MRAs this one defaults to 00, not ff, and the core
      passes bit 15 straight through instead of inverting it.
    -->
    <switches default="00" base="0">
        <dip name="Flip Screen"  bits="0" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="1" ids="Off,On"/>
    </switches>
</misterromdescription>
